{"id":"m5-l2","title":"Instruction sequencing","aspect":0.5625,"wpt":960,"slides":[{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0348,0.2599,0.8589,0.2401],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Instruction and Instruction Sequencing","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"ctr"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"1","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.1236,0.5907,0.7398,0.16],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Carl Hamacher, Zvonko ","sz":20.0,"b":1},{"text":"Vranesic","sz":20.0,"b":1},{"text":" and Safwat Zaky, Computer Organization and Embedded Systems, (6e), McGraw Hill Publication, 2017.","sz":20.0,"b":1}],"align":"just"}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Instruction and Instruction Sequencing","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"2","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.9758,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"The tasks carried out by a computer program consist of a sequence of small steps, such","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"as adding two numbers, testing for a particular condition, reading a character from the","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"keyboard, or sending a character to be displayed on a display screen. ","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"A computer must have instructions capable of performing four types of operations:","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"An instruction specifies an operation to be performed and the operands involved.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"\u201cMust-Perform\u201d Operations","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Data transfers between the memory and the processor registers","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Arithmetic and logic operations on data","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Program sequencing and control","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"I/O transfers","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Register Transfer Notation","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"3","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.825,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Identify a location by a symbolic name standing for its hardware binary address (LOC, R0,\u2026)","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Contents of a location are denoted by placing square brackets around the name of the location  ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"e.g.  \tR1\u2190[LOC]","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"\tR3 \u2190[R1]+[R2]","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"This type of notation is Register Transfer Notation (RTN)","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Assembly-Language Notation","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"4","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.9758,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Represent machine instructions and programs.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Move R1,LOC  ","sz":24.0},{"text":"\u2192 ","sz":24.0},{"text":"R1\u2190[LOC]","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Add R3, R1, R2  ","sz":24.0},{"text":"\u2192 ","sz":24.0},{"text":"R3 \u2190[R1]+[R2]","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"RISC and CISC Instruction Set","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"5","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0121,0.1028,0.9758,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Most important characteristics that distinguish different computers is the nature of their instructions","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"Reduced Instruction Set Computers (RISC)","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Higher performance can be achieved if each instruction occupies exactly one word in memory, and all operands needed to execute a given arithmetic or logic operation specified by an instruction are already in processor registers","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Various operations needed to process a sequence of instructions are performed in \u201cpipelined\u201d fashion","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Number of different types of instructions may be included in the instruction set of a computer.","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Complex Instruction Set Computers (CISC)","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"An alternative to RISC makes use of more complex instructions which may span more than one word of memory,  and","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"may specify more complicated operations.","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Introduction to RISC Instruction Sets","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"6","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0242,0.1028,0.9758,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Two key characteristics of RISC instruction sets are:","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Each instruction fits in a single word.","sz":20.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"A load/store architecture is used, in which","sz":20.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Memory operands are accessed only using Load and Store instructions.","sz":20.0}],"lvl":2,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"All operands involved in an arithmetic or logic operation must either be in processor registers, or one of the operands may be given explicitly within the instruction word.","sz":20.0}],"lvl":2,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"At the start of execution of a program, all instructions and data used in the program are stored in the memory of a computer.","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Processor registers do not contain valid operands at that time. ","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load instructions: Operands expected to be in processor registers before they can be used by an instruction, are transferred from memory location into the registers.","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load instructions are of the form","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load destination, source","sz":20.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"In general,","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load ","sz":20.0},{"text":"processor_register","sz":20.0},{"text":", ","sz":20.0},{"text":"memory_location","sz":20.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.825,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"RISC Instruction Sets","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"7","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0242,0.1698,0.9758,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"C = A + B","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"C \u2190 [A] + [B]","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Assembly:","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load","sz":24.0},{"text":" R2, A","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Load","sz":24.0},{"text":" R3, B","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Add","sz":24.0},{"text":" R4, R2, R3","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Store R4, C","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Three-Address Instructions","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Add destination, source1, source2","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The Store instruction is of the form","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Store source, destination","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.5053,0.6625,0.5107,0.2124],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Two-Address Instructions","sz":24.0,"c":"#3F3F3F"}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Add R3, R2, R3","sz":24.0,"c":"#3F3F3F"}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Store R3, C","sz":24.0,"c":"#3F3F3F"}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.9615,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Instruction Execution and Straight-Line Sequencing","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.4599,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"a possible program segment for the task, C=A+B, as it appears in the memory of a computer. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"word length is 32 bits","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Straight-line sequencing: using the information in the PC to fetch and execute instructions, one at a time, in the order of increasing addresses.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Executing a given instruction is a two-phase procedure. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Instruction fetch (First phase)","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Instruction execute (Second phase)","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"8","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.4821,0.2029,0.4981,0.7728],"src":"/dsco/slides/m5-l2/1.webp"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.9615,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Branching","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.2509,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Consider the task of adding a list of n numbers.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"9","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.3964,0.0082,0.4929,0.9808],"src":"/dsco/slides/m5-l2/2.webp"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.9615,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Branching","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.1893,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Consider the task of adding a list of n numbers in a loop.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"10","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.367,0.0393,0.4824,0.9215],"src":"/dsco/slides/m5-l2/3.webp"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.9615,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Branching","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.8353,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Branch_if","sz":24.0},{"text":"_[R4]>[R5] LOOP","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"In generic assembly language as:\t","sz":24.0},{"text":"Branch_greater_than","sz":24.0},{"text":" R4, R5, LOOP","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Using an actual mnemonic as:\t\tBGT R4, R5, LOOP","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1,"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"11","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0,0.0022,0.9615,0.2347],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Generating Memory Addresses","sz":44.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0044,0.2029,0.4956,0.7781],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"The purpose of the instruction block starting at LOOP is to add successive numbers from the list during each pass through the loop","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"must refer to a different address during each pass","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"memory operand address cannot be given directly in a single Load instruction in the loop.","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"As one possibility, suppose that a processor register, Ri, is used to hold the memory address of an operand. If it is initially loaded with the address NUM1 before the loop is entered and is then incremented by 4 on each pass through the loop, it can provide the needed capability.","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"This situation, and many others like it, give rise to the need for flexible ways to specify the address of an operand. ","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The instruction set of a computer typically provides a number of such methods, called addressing modes.","sz":20.4}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.7046,0.8809,0.056,0.0532],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"12","sz":18.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.4992,0.2084,0.4278,0.7895],"src":"/dsco/slides/m5-l2/3.webp"}]}]}